<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wenzel Anton von Kaunitz-Rietberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1711">1711</a>–<a href="/years/1794">1794</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Wenzel Anton, Prince of Kaunitz-Rietberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wenzel Anton von Kaunitz-Rietberg was state chancellor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarchy from 1753 to 1792 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>, Joseph II and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Leopold II</a>. He engineered the alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> of 1756, the Diplomatic Revolution, and his family held the small Westphalian County of Rietberg through marriage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wenzel-anton-von-kaunitz-rietberg.jpg" alt="Wenzel Anton von Kaunitz-Rietberg" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jean-Étienne Liotard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean-Etienne_Liotard_12.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wenzel Anton was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on 2 February 1711, one of 19 children of Maximilian Ulrich, Count of Kaunitz, and Maria Ernestine von Rietberg, an heiress of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-cirksena">House of Cirksena</a>. The Kaunitz family belonged to the ancient Bohemian nobility, and through his mother's marriage portion the family inherited the immediate County of Rietberg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a>. As the second son he was first meant for the church and held a canonry at Münster at thirteen, but after his elder brother died he studied law and diplomacy at Vienna, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a>.</p><p>He became a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulic-council">Imperial Aulic Council</a> in 1735, served as ambassador at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> from 1742 and from 1744 as minister plenipotentiary in the Austrian Netherlands, and he signed the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle on 23 October 1748 reluctantly, since it confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in Silesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klodzko">Glatz</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Kaunitz had long opposed the alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> that had existed since 1731. He argued to Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick II of Prussia</a> was the most wicked and dangerous enemy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and that Silesia could be regained only by an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. She entrusted him with the execution of this plan, and as ambassador at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> from 1750 to 1752 he prepared it. As State Chancellor from 1753 to 1792 he reorganised the foreign office on the Ballhausplatz.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> of 1756, the Diplomatic Revolution, moved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> from an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> allied with Britain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, and this lineup framed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>, which did not bring Silesia back. In 1761 he founded the Austrian Council of State, and after the war he became a Prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><p>Under Joseph II, whose reforms Kaunitz found too fast, the two quarrelled and Kaunitz several times offered his resignation. He backed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772 and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innviertel">Innviertel</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cieszyn">Teschen</a> of 1779. He worked to subject the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> to the state, and he patronised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christoph-willibald-gluck">Christoph Willibald Gluck</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfgang-amadeus-mozart">Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart</a>. He pressed for the Austro-Turkish War of 1788–1791, which proved costly and gained no anti-Prussian aim.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Joseph II died, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Leopold II</a> blamed Kaunitz for the war's failure and sharply restricted his powers. Kaunitz resigned on the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> in July 1792 and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> in 1794. He was buried in the family vault at Slavkov. His granddaughter Eleonora married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klemens-von-metternich">Klemens von Metternich</a>, who later held the same office.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wenzel Anton, Prince of Kaunitz-Rietberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Through marriage it passed in the 16th century to the East Frisian Cirksena family and then, in 1699, to the Kaunitz family, whose most famous member, <b>Wenzel Anton von Kaunitz-Rietberg</b>, became Austrian state chancellor under Maria Theresa.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-rietberg">County of Rietberg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in Mariahilf, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1764" aria-label="Vienna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Wenzel Anton von Kaunitz-Rietberg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1764">What was here in 1764? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
